{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-331872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that  $L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-{3}^{2}}{((s-4)^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a00194a376d3e0a6746015e9008f5e5e35581856d7f79571d604fe69cef85a7"} {"question" "q-331872" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331872" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{(s+4)^{2}-9}{((s+4)^{2}+9)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-4}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{(s-4)^{2}+9}"] ["math" "\\frac{{s}^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331872" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{2\\omega s}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)}=\\frac{2(5)s}{({s}^{2}+{5}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)} & =\\frac{2(5)(s-3)}{((s-3)^{2}+{5}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(s-3)}{((s-3)^{2}+25)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a00194a376d3e0a6746015e9008f5e5e35581856d7f79571d604fe69cef85a7"} {"question" "q-331864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{10(s+3)}{((s+3)^{2}+25)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{10(s-3)}{((s-3)^{2}+25)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{10(s-3)}{((s-3)^{2}+9)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{10(s-3)}{(s-3)^{2}+25}"] ["math" "\\frac{10s}{((s-3)^{2}+25)^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10(s-3)}{((s-3)^{2}+25)^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{6}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)} & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a00194a376d3e0a6746015e9008f5e5e35581856d7f79571d604fe69cef85a7"} {"question" "q-331860" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331860" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}"] ["math" "\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+6}"] ["math" "\\frac{s+3}{(s-3)^{2}+36}"] ["math" "\\frac{s-3}{(s-3)^{2}+36}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{(s+3)^{2}+36}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331860" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-325858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot {t}^{3}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-325858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{3}}=\\frac{3!}{{s}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot {t}^{3}} & =\\frac{3!}{(s-4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(s-4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-325858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a00194a376d3e0a6746015e9008f5e5e35581856d7f79571d604fe69cef85a7"} {"question" "q-325858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-325858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3}{(s+4)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{(s-4)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{(s+3)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{(s-3)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{(s-4)^{4}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-325858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6}{(s-4)^{4}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{4}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{4}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)} & =\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-{4}^{2}}{((s-3)^{2}+{4}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-16}{((s-3)^{2}+16)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a00194a376d3e0a6746015e9008f5e5e35581856d7f79571d604fe69cef85a7"} {"question" "q-331869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{(s+3)^{2}-16}{((s+3)^{2}+16)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-16}{((s-3)^{2}+16)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-16}{(s-3)^{2}+16}"] ["math" "\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-4}{((s-3)^{2}+16)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{s}^{2}-16}{((s-3)^{2}+16)^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-16}{((s-3)^{2}+16)^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=5{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{5{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{5{e}^{-t}}+L{3{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =5L{{e}^{-t}}+3L{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{6}{(s-2)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =5L{{e}^{-t}}+3L{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+1}+3\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s-2)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{18}{(s-2)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a00194a376d3e0a6746015e9008f5e5e35581856d7f79571d604fe69cef85a7"} {"question" "q-332190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{18}{(s+2)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{18}{(s-2)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{6}{(s-2)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{s-1}+\\frac{18}{(s+2)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{s-1}+\\frac{18}{(s-2)^{4}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{18}{(s-2)^{4}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-89592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-89592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+L{2t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+2L{t{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+2L{t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-89592" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a00194a376d3e0a6746015e9008f5e5e35581856d7f79571d604fe69cef85a7"} {"question" "q-89592" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-89592" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{(s+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s-2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{(s+2)(s-2)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{(s+2)^{2}(s-2)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{(s+2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s-2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-89592" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=5{t}^{2}+9t{e}^{3t}$ for $s>3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{5{t}^{2}+9t{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =L{5{t}^{2}}+L{9t{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =5L{{t}^{2}}+9L{t{e}^{3t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nL{t{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{1}{(s-3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =5L{{t}^{2}}+9L{t{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+9\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{(s-3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a00194a376d3e0a6746015e9008f5e5e35581856d7f79571d604fe69cef85a7"} {"question" "q-332133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{(s-3)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{(s-3)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{(s-3)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{(s-3)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{(s-3)^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{(s-3)^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332199" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}$ for $s>2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332199" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =L{6{e}^{2t}}+L{2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =6L{{e}^{2t}}+2L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} & =\\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =6L{{e}^{2t}}+2L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}+2\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332199" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a00194a376d3e0a6746015e9008f5e5e35581856d7f79571d604fe69cef85a7"} {"question" "q-332199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{s+2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s-4)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}$ for $s>4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{4t}}+L{5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{4t}}+5L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} & =\\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{4t}}+5L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+5\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a00194a376d3e0a6746015e9008f5e5e35581856d7f79571d604fe69cef85a7"} {"question" "q-332183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{s+4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s-1)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-332183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-328557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{3t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-328557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{3t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the right." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-328557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5a00194a376d3e0a6746015e9008f5e5e35581856d7f79571d604fe69cef85a7"} {"question" "q-328557" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-328557" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $3$ horizontally."] ["text" "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $3$ vertically."] ["text" "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the left."] ["text" "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the right."] ["text" "The Laplace transform is unchanged."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-328557" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the right."] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differen