rade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331788" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331788" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "G(s)"] ["math" "G(s+3)"] ["math" "G(s-3)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331788" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "G(s+3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331788" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331788" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "s>{s}_{0}"] ["math" "s>{s}_{0}+3"] ["math" "s>{s}_{0}-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "q-331788" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "s>{s}_{0}-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a516a99ebdb67ce5af57eb2ee27b6e39e7c23f318e5ad08f9e363fa48dbe171"} {"question" "e-21424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{t\\sin(\\omega t) \\right\\}=\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^2},$ find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-5t}\\cdot t\\sin(2t)\\right\\}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/example-21424.json" "file_sha256" "2c97a9749bfab67adf71adc752c9f62ceddaeef2606ccd18143f78b6ba61e0c4"} {"question" "e-21424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$\nthen\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{t\\sin(2t) \\right\\} = \\frac{2(2)s}{(s^{2}+2^2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-5t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} & =\\frac{2(2)(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+{2}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s+5)}{((s+5)^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/example-21424.json" "file_sha256" "2c97a9749bfab67adf71adc752c9f62ceddaeef2606ccd18143f78b6ba61e0c4"} {"question" "e-21590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $g(t) = 2t^2 + 5te^{4t}$ for $s > 4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/example-21590.json" "file_sha256" "1bfa19c724e276284470dadf2d531ff82d66019b4d006a92ca46072262e5d5f1"} {"question" "e-21590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{2{t}^{2}+5t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{2{t}^{2}}+L{5t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =2L{{t}^{2}}+5L{t{e}^{4t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$ then $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ te^{4t} \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{(s-4)^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $g(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =2L{{t}^{2}}+5L{t{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{(s-4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/example-21590.json" "file_sha256" "1bfa19c724e276284470dadf2d531ff82d66019b4d006a92ca46072262e5d5f1"} {"question" "e-21549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/example-21549.json" "file_sha256" "a256e497c2e7cae4d92d92d68461b12d07d4521c7f804c46f69fc74c7ca30318"} {"question" "e-21549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),$\nthen\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by $e^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a > 0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a < 0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/example-21549.json" "file_sha256" "a256e497c2e7cae4d92d92d68461b12d07d4521c7f804c46f69fc74c7ca30318"} {"question" "e-21531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$ for $s>s_0.$ Suppose we wish to find $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-7t} f(t)\\}.$ Show the steps to compute this transform." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/example-21531.json" "file_sha256" "1154619f0dd91f60826559c4496c03873b414b1208125236b16ef9e0e82b00ee"} {"question" "e-21531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-7t}f(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-7t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+7)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t,$ where $s > s_0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns + 7 > s_0\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \ns > s_0 - 7.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/example-21531.json" "file_sha256" "1154619f0dd91f60826559c4496c03873b414b1208125236b16ef9e0e82b00ee"}] "usage" {"q-325858" [] "e-21424" [] "e-21531" [] "e-21590" [] "e-21549" [] "q-328557" [] "q-89592" [] "q-328705" []} "basis" 2666 "retractions" [[17592186221863 :question/problem "Given that $L{t^{n}} = \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}$, find the Laplace transform $L{e^{4t} \\cdot t^{3}}$."] [17592186221984 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$L{t^{n}}|= \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}, \\mid s > 0$,\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-2t} + 2te^{2t}$ for $s > 2$."] [17592186221866 :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)} = F(s)$. How can we obtain the graph of $L{e^{3t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)$?"] [17592186221867 :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L{f(t)} = F(s)$ for $s > s_{0}$. Suppose we wish to find $L{e^{2t}f(t)}$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$e^{-st} \\cdot e^{2t}f(t)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}}"] [17592186221096 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{t\\sin(\\omega t) \\right\\}=\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^2}$, find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-5t}\\cdot t\\sin(2t)\\right\\}$."] [17592186221096 :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\} = F(s-a)\n$$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{t\\sin(2t) \\right\\} = \\frac{2(2)s}{(s^{2}+2^2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{-5t} \\cdot t\\sin (2t)} &= (2(2)(s + 5))/(((s + 5)^{2} + 2^{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4(s + 5))/(((s + 5)^{2} + 4)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186221108 :question/problem "Given that\n$L{t^{n}}|= \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}, \\mid s > 0$,\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $g(t) = 2t^2 + 5te^{4t}$ for $s > 4$."] [17592186221108 :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t)} &= L{2t^{2} + 5te^{4t}} \\\\\n&= L{2t^{2}} + L{5te^{4t}} \\\\\n&= 2L{t^{2}} + 5L{te^{4t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s > 0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{t^{n}} &= \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{t} &= \\frac{1}{s^{2}} \\\\\nL{t^{2}} &= \\frac{2}{s^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\} = F(s-a)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{te^{4t} \\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{(s-4)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $g(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t)} &= 2L{t^{2}} + 5L{te^{4t}} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{s^{3}} + 5 \\cdot (1)/((s - 4)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{s^{3}} + (5)/((s - 4)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4$."] [17592186221103 :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$. How can we obtain the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)$?"] [17592186221103 :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\} = F(s-a)\n$$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by $e^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a > 0$, the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n- If $a < 0$, the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-6t} f(t)\\}$, we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left."] [17592186221100 :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$ for $s>s_0$. Suppose we wish to find $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-7t} f(t)\\}$. Show the steps to compute this transform."] [17592186221100 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{-7t}f(t)} &= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st} \\cdot e^{-7t}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{- (s + 7)t}f(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $s > s_0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7)\n$$\n\nwhere\n$s + 7 > s_0 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad s > s_0 - 7$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186220085 17592186220086 17592186220087 17592186220088 17592186220089 17592186220103 17592186220104 17592186220105 17592186220106 17592186220107 17592186220109 17592186220110 17592186220111 17592186220112 17592186220114 17592186220115 17592186220116 17592186220117 17592186220119 17592186220120 17592186220121 17592186220123 17592186220124 17592186220125 17592186220893 17592186220894 17592186220895 17592186220896 17592186220897] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220084 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220084 :answer-field/id] [17592186220084 :answer-field/key] [17592186220084 :answer-field/type] [17592186220102 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220102 :answer-field/id] [17592186220102 :answer-field/key] [17592186220102 :answer-field/type] [17592186220108 :answer-field/id] [17592186220108 :answer-field/key] [17592186220108 :answer-field/type] [17592186220113 :answer-field/id] [17592186220113 :answer-field/key] [17592186220113 :answer-field/type] [17592186220118 :answer-field/id] [17592186220118 :answer-field/key] [17592186220118 :answer-field/type] [17592186220122 :answer-field/id] [17592186220122 :answer-field/key] [17592186220122 :answer-field/type] [17592186220892 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220892 :answer-field/id] [17592186220892 :answer-field/key] [17592186220892 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
